def year12_decision():
    data=[
    ('spanning','Trees, spanning trees and connector costs','4.3.1,4.3.3','network',
     'Define a tree::A tree is a connected undirected graph with no cycles. A tree on v vertices has v−1 edges. Between each pair there is exactly one simple path; removing any edge disconnects it.||Span the required vertices::A spanning tree contains every vertex of the original connected graph using a subset of its edges. It need not use every original edge. Check that no required location is omitted and that the chosen links create no cycle.||Calculate connector cost::Sum the chosen edge weights once. This objective connects all locations, rather than minimising one endpoint-to-endpoint route. A long route within the tree does not automatically mean the total connector is expensive.||Distinguish a minimum::Many spanning trees can exist with different costs. A valid tree is not necessarily minimal; compare candidates or use a justified minimum-spanning procedure. Ties may allow multiple minimum trees.',
     'Any connected set of edges is a tree even if it has a cycle.','A tree is connected and cycle-free.||For v vertices it has v−1 edges.||A cyclic connected selection includes a redundant connector and is not a tree.'),
    ('prim','Prim’s algorithm and minimum spanning trees','4.3.2,4.3.3','network',
     'Start a reached set::Choose a starting vertex. Prim grows one connected set, repeatedly selecting a cheapest edge from a reached vertex to an unreached vertex. State tie choices when equal eligible weights occur.||Use only eligible edges::An edge entirely within the reached set creates a cycle and is excluded. An edge joining two unreached vertices does not grow the current tree and is not yet eligible. Eligibility is as important as small weight.||Finish and total::Stop when every vertex is reached; the tree has v−1 edges. Sum their original weights and state the resulting practical connector cost. Record each chosen edge so the process can be audited.||Explain the objective::Prim finds minimum total spanning connection, not shortest travel from the start. Ties may produce different equally minimal trees. Compare required locations and any prohibited links with the model assumptions.',
     'Prim selects the cheapest unused edge anywhere in the graph at each step.','Prim selects the cheapest eligible edge crossing from reached to unreached vertices.||An isolated cheap edge or an internal edge is not eligible.||The reached-set condition preserves a connected cycle-free growing tree.'),
    ('projectnetwork','Activity dependencies and project networks','4.3.4','project',
     'State the representation::These lessons use activity-on-node networks: each node is a task and arrows show prerequisites. Durations label activities. The same dependency problem can also use activity-on-arrow, but conventions must not be mixed silently.||Add every prerequisite::An activity with two predecessors waits for both. Include arrows from each predecessor and do not replace this with an either-or relationship. Starting activities link from Start and final tasks link to Finish.||Separate time from arrows::An arrow in an activity-on-node model represents a dependency, not an extra duration. Nodes may represent concurrent work. A diagram’s spatial length does not measure how long the task takes.||State resource assumptions::Basic critical-path analysis assumes activities can run concurrently unless dependence prevents it. Shared staff or equipment can impose additional constraints. The calculated earliest duration is conditional on the stated scheduling model.',
     'An activity with two predecessors can start when either one finishes.','It must wait for all required predecessors to finish.||Both dependency arrows encode necessary conditions.||Using either-or timing can produce an impossible project schedule.'),
    ('forward','Forward scanning and earliest starts','4.3.5','project',
     'Begin at time zero::A starting activity has earliest start zero; earliest finish equals its start plus duration. Use a consistent day-zero convention. Calendar dates can be mapped afterward if the task requires them.||Take the maximum at a merge::EST of an activity is the maximum earliest finish of all predecessors. It cannot begin until the latest of them finishes. A minimum would ignore an unfinished prerequisite.||Propagate in dependency order::Process predecessors before successors. A valid project dependency network is acyclic; a circular dependency prevents this simple scheduling order and needs clarification. Show starts and finishes in a table.||Read completion::The earliest project finish is the latest earliest finish among required terminal activities. It is a minimum under the concurrency assumptions, not a guaranteed calendar completion including unmodelled delays.',
     'At a merge use the earliest predecessor finish because one completed task is enough.','Use the maximum finish time across all required predecessors.||Every prerequisite must be complete.||Choosing the minimum can start an activity prematurely.'),
    ('backward','Backward scanning and latest starts','4.3.5','project',
     'Fix the completion target::Backward scanning uses a stated project finish, normally the earliest finish from the forward pass. Set each required terminal task’s latest finish to that target. A later deadline changes calculated floats.||Subtract duration::LST=LFT−duration. Work backward only after successor times are available. A latest start is the final time an activity can begin without violating the chosen finish target under the model.||Take the minimum at a split::An activity must finish by the earliest latest-start requirement among all its successors. Use the minimum successor LST, not maximum. Otherwise one branch can miss its allowable start.||Check both passes::Latest starts should not precede earliest starts for a feasible target. Calculate total float as LST−EST. A negative value flags an infeasible deadline or inconsistent pass, rather than usable negative spare time.',
     'At a split use the maximum successor latest start.','Use the minimum latest start among required successors.||The predecessor must be ready for the tightest successor requirement.||Using a maximum can delay one branch beyond its allowable start.'),
    ('critical','Critical paths and multiple longest chains','4.3.6,4.3.7','project',
     'Identify zero total float::An activity is critical under the current duration model when LST=EST. Critical activities link into start-to-finish paths whose total duration equals project completion. Check connections rather than joining all zero-float tasks arbitrarily.||Find the longest dependency chain::The critical path determines the minimum feasible project duration because its tasks cannot overlap along that path. It is a longest duration path in this scheduling network, not a shortest route problem.||Recognise multiple paths::Two different chains can share the longest duration. Delaying either can delay completion. Shortening only one may leave the other as the binding chain; recompute the schedule before claiming a project-wide gain.||Respond to changes::A duration change can move the critical path and change floats. The previous critical list is not permanent. State that the basic model assumes unchanged dependencies and adequate resources for concurrent tasks.',
     'A project can have only one critical path.','Several dependency chains can tie for the longest duration.||Every tied critical path constrains completion.||Recompute all paths or start/finish times when durations change.'),
    ('float','Total float and interacting delays','4.3.8','project',
     'Compute total float::Total float is LST−EST, equivalently LFT−EFT for the same activity. It measures a maximum delay under the current schedule and completion target. Zero means critical; positive float indicates some scheduling flexibility.||Distinguish types of float::Free float is delay before affecting a successor’s earliest start. Total float can use slack elsewhere on the chain without delaying overall completion. These are not automatically equal, and the tasks here explicitly ask for total float.||Avoid independent allowances::Activities on a shared noncritical chain can share its slack. Delaying one may consume flexibility attributed to another. Do not add every listed float as if all could be used independently.||Check an actual delay::Recalculate affected starts and finishes after proposed changes. Report which target is preserved and what assumptions remain. A resource conflict may impose a stronger limit than precedence-only total float.',
     'Every activity can simultaneously use its full total float independently.','Float allowances can interact along shared paths.||One delay can consume slack available to another activity.||Recompute the proposed schedule rather than adding independent-looking allowances.'),
    ('flow','Maximum flow and minimum cuts','4.3.9','flow',
     'Respect capacity and direction::A flow on each directed arc is nonnegative and no greater than its capacity. At intermediate vertices, total inflow equals total outflow. The source and sink are the only permitted net suppliers and receivers in these tasks.||Build a feasible flow::Send flow along source-to-sink routes without exceeding remaining capacities. Shared arcs constrain simultaneous routes. Record each arc flow and check conservation, rather than summing capacities as if all were independently usable.||Use a cut as a bound::A cut partitions vertices into a source side and sink side. Its directed capacity sums arcs from the source side to the sink side. Any feasible total flow cannot exceed this capacity; reverse-crossing arcs are not added to that bound.||Prove optimality::Exhibit a feasible flow whose value equals a cut capacity. Equality gives a maximum-flow and minimum-cut certificate. A large feasible flow without a matching upper bound does not yet prove optimality.',
     'The sum of all capacities in the network is always its maximum flow.','Maximum flow is constrained by direction, conservation and bottlenecks.||A separating cut provides an upper bound.||Prove the maximum with a feasible flow equal to a cut capacity.'),
    ('assignment','Bipartite assignments and small-case optimisation','4.3.10,4.3.11','assignment',
     'Separate the two sets::A bipartite assignment model places workers and jobs in different groups. A one-to-one assignment chooses one worker per job and one job per worker. The matrix’s row and column labels define the cost of each pairing.||Enumerate small cases::For three workers and three jobs there are six complete assignments. Compare their full costs, retaining all ties. A candidate that repeats a column is not feasible, even if it has a small sum.||Avoid independent row choices::Two workers’ cheapest options can be the same job. Local choices ignore the one-to-one constraint. Compare whole matchings rather than taking a row minimum independently.||Choose the objective::Costs are minimised; productivity or benefit may be maximised. Prohibited assignments need an explicit restriction. If group sizes differ, explain any dummy rows or columns used and their cost meaning rather than silently padding the matrix.',
     'Selecting each worker’s cheapest job independently guarantees a valid optimum.','A feasible assignment must use distinct jobs.||Independent row minima can repeat the same column.||Optimise the total over complete one-to-one matchings.'),
    ('hungarian','Hungarian reductions and optimality','4.3.11','assignment',
     'Reduce rows and columns::Subtract each row’s minimum from that row, then each column’s minimum from that column. Every complete assignment uses one entry from each row and column, so these subtractions change all assignment totals by the same amount.||Seek independent zeros::Choose zeros in distinct rows and columns. For an n×n reduced nonnegative matrix, n independent zeros give a zero reduced total and establish a minimum. One zero somewhere in every row is insufficient if columns conflict.||Adjust when necessary::Cover all zeros with a minimum number of row/column lines. If fewer than n lines suffice, subtract the smallest uncovered entry from uncovered cells and add it at intersections of two lines; singly covered cells stay unchanged. Repeat and seek independent zeros.||Recover the original cost::Report the assignment using original labels and calculate its cost from the original matrix. For maximisation, first convert benefits to costs consistently, such as subtracting each benefit from a common maximum. The original benefit total is then the reported objective.',
     'The optimum original cost is always zero once independent reduced zeros are selected.','Reduced zeros establish the assignment but original entries determine its reported cost.||Row and column reductions remove common constants.||Recover the original total instead of treating reduced cost as actual cost.'),
    ]
    for key,title,ids,diagram,notes,wrong,correction in data:L(4,key,title,ids,notes,wrong,correction,diagram,'assignment' if key in ['assignment','hungarian'] else None)

